                    round_name_r4:          `Publish & Defend`,

                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Frame the valuation problem`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Lumengrid's prospectus is on the desk: €132M revenue growing 38%, an 81% gross margin, a €9M operating loss — and only <strong>€6M of net tangible assets</strong> against a €1.4–1.9bn ask. The trap is to call it overpriced on book value or the loss. As Dubreuille argues, the value is <strong>intangible</strong> — algorithms, data, a 1,900-customer base at 124% net revenue retention — and the accounts barely carry it. Choose a <em>weighted toolkit</em> (DCF, revenue/gross-profit multiples, sum-of-the-parts, a real-options lens), avoid betting everything on one method, and state the question your institutional client actually needs answered.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Weight the intangibles`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Put a <strong>defensible</strong> value on what the accounts miss. Build the 1,900-customer base as an asset from cohort economics — retention, expansion, gross margin, churn and a discount rate — rather than a round number per logo. Treat the €71M of expensed R&D as <strong>investment with an economic rationale</strong>, not a textbook ten-year amortisation. Translate the algorithm/data moat into a growth-durability premium. And guard against the killer error: an intangible shows up in <em>cash flows</em>, so carry each one through exactly one method and do not double-count value the revenue multiple already prices in.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Set assumptions & run the range`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `A valuation is a range and an argument, not a number. Set the base-case DCF — <strong>WACC, terminal growth, terminal margin</strong> — knowing a 1-pt WACC move or a 2-pt terminal-growth move shifts equity value by hundreds of €millions. Reconcile it against the multiple-based value, then run <strong>bear/base/bull</strong> and name the single assumption that carries the most risk. Decide where your central estimate sits versus the €1.4–1.9bn IPO range — landing there honestly is fine; <em>back-solving to fit the bankers</em> is not.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Publish the recommendation`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Two days before publication the curveballs land: a <strong>rival AI energy platform</strong> launches, pressuring your growth-durability assumption — the biggest driver of your number — and your largest customer (8% of revenue) is <strong>reviewing its contract</strong>. Then Aurial's advisory head hints a <em>“constructive” note would help the banking relationship</em>. Re-test the valuation against the news, protect your independence under MiFID II, and settle a rating, price target and 12-month horizon you can defend live to the investment committee. Credibility is the one asset an analyst truly owns.`,

                    r1_lens_label:          `A junior writes “€6M of net assets, an operating loss, €1.6bn ask — this is absurdly overpriced.” What is your team's stance?`,
                    r1_weight_label:        `Allocate your valuation weight across methods (must total 100%)`,
                    r1_weight_readout:      `Weight allocated:`,
                    r1_weight_cap:          `% / 100%`,
                    r1_question_label:      `How sharply does the team state the question the recommendation must answer for an institutional client? (%)`,
                    r1_question_readout:    `Clarity of the investment question:`,
                    lens_intangible:        `The value is intangible — algorithms, data, a 124%-retention base — and must be valued on its own terms, not dismissed`,
                    lens_bookvalue:         `Book value rules — €6M of net assets means the company is worth roughly €6M`,
                    lens_earnings:          `No earnings means no value — a loss-making firm cannot justify €1.6bn`,
                    lens_momentum:          `The market sets the price — just publish the midpoint of the bankers' range`,
                    m_dcf:                  `DCF on long-horizon cash flows (captures growth durability)`,
                    m_multiple:             `Revenue / gross-profit multiples vs. SaaS comparables`,
                    m_sotp:                 `Sum-of-the-parts (platform + customer base + data assets)`,
                    m_realopt:              `Real-options lens on the platform's optionality`,

                    r2_clv_label:           `How do you value the 1,900-customer base (124% net revenue retention) as an asset?`,
                    r2_rd_label:            `How do you treat the €71M of cumulatively expensed R&D?`,
                    r2_double_label:        `You valued the customer base standalone AND applied a 22× revenue multiple. How do you handle double-counting?`,
                    r2_moat_label:          `Translate the algorithm/data moat and switching costs into a growth-durability premium (% added to fade-adjusted growth)`,
                    r2_moat_hint:           `An intangible is only worth its cash-flow consequence — a real moat slows the growth fade; an imagined one inflates the number.`,
                    clv_cohort:             `Cohort lifetime-value build — retention, expansion, gross margin and a discount rate drive the cash flows`,
                    clv_headcount:          `A flat figure per customer × 1,900 — a round number is good enough under deadline`,
                    clv_ignore:             `Ignore it — recurring revenue is already in the income statement`,
                    rd_economic:            `Capitalise the portion that built a durable asset, with an amortisation life justified by the platform's economics`,
                    rd_mechanical:          `Capitalise all €71M and amortise over ten years because the textbook says so`,
                    rd_leave:               `Leave it as expense — IFRS already expensed it, so the asset isn't real`,
                    double_oneroute:        `Carry each intangible's cash flow through exactly one method and net out the overlap`,
                    double_both:            `Keep both — a higher number is easier to defend as “buy”`,
                    double_neither:         `Drop the intangible work entirely and rely on the multiple alone`,

                    r3_wacc_label:          `Discount rate (WACC) — ±1 pt moves the value by hundreds of €millions (%)`,
                    r3_wacc_readout:        `WACC:`,
                    r3_wacc_unit:           `%`,
                    r3_tg_label:            `Terminal growth — ±2 pts moves the value by hundreds of €millions (%)`,
                    r3_tg_readout:          `Terminal growth:`,
                    r3_tg_unit:             `%`,
                    r3_margin_label:        `Terminal operating margin once growth investment normalises (%)`,
                    r3_output_label:        `How do you present the valuation to the desk?`,
