                    warn_weight_negative:   `Method weights cannot be negative.`,
                    warn_weight_total:      `Your method weights must total exactly 100%. Re-balance.`,
                    warn_question_range:    `Investment-question clarity must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_moat_range:        `The growth-durability premium must be between 0% and 20%.`,
                    warn_wacc_range:        `WACC must be between 7% and 14%.`,
                    warn_tg_range:          `Terminal growth must be between 1% and 6%.`,
                    warn_margin_range:      `Terminal margin must be between 5% and 40%.`,
                    warn_tg_wacc:           `Terminal growth must be below the WACC, or the Gordon model breaks. Lower terminal growth or raise the discount rate.`,
                    warn_defence_range:     `IC-defence conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_intangible:    `<strong>That's the right frame.</strong> You refused to dismiss the company on €6M of book value and an accounting loss. The value is intangible — algorithms, data, a 124%-retention base — and your job is to value it on its own terms, not to wish it away.`,
                    coach_r1_bookvalue:     `<strong>Book-value anchoring.</strong> By that logic almost every software company that ever IPO'd was worthless at listing. The €6M of net assets is precisely <em>not</em> where Lumengrid's value lives. Where is the intangible value in your model?`,
                    coach_r1_earnings:      `<strong>“No earnings, no value” misreads a growth company.</strong> The loss is reinvestment into R&D and sales that builds the intangible base. A high-growth, 81%-gross-margin SaaS firm is valued on durable future cash flows, not this year's reported loss.`,
                    coach_r1_momentum:      `<strong>Publishing the bankers' midpoint isn't analysis.</strong> Your institutional clients pay for an <em>independent</em> view, not an echo of the sponsor range. If you can't say why the number is right, you have no recommendation.`,
                    coach_r1_onemethod:     `<strong>One method is fragile.</strong> A loss-making, high-growth firm can't be valued on a single lens — DCF alone over-relies on terminal assumptions, multiples alone import comparables' flaws. Triangulate so no single weak assumption blows up the answer.`,
                    coach_r1_speculative:   `<strong>Real options are a sanity check, not the engine.</strong> Leaning the valuation on platform optionality invites you to justify almost any number. Anchor on DCF and multiples and use real options only at the margin.`,
                    coach_r1_triangulated:  `<strong>Well triangulated.</strong> DCF and multiples carry the weight, with sum-of-the-parts and real options as cross-checks. That's how you stay “roughly right” when any one assumption is wrong — exactly the discipline this valuation needs.`,
                    coach_r1_noquestion:    `<strong>What question are you actually answering?</strong> Your client doesn't want a number, they want to know whether to buy the IPO at €22–28. Frame the decision sharply or the whole model floats free of the point.`,
                    coach_r2_cohort:        `<strong>Cohort economics is the right build.</strong> Valuing the base on retention, expansion, margin and churn turns a 124%-NRR customer relationship into an explicit, defensible asset — instead of a hand-waved number per logo.`,
                    coach_r2_headcount:     `<strong>A flat figure per logo isn't a valuation.</strong> 1,900 customers at 124% net revenue retention are not interchangeable — expansion and churn dynamics drive the cash flows. Build the cohort, even roughly, or you're guessing.`,
                    coach_r2_ignore:        `<strong>You've ignored the single biggest intangible.</strong> A 124%-retention base is the core of the value. “It's already in revenue” is only true if your DCF actually models the expansion — otherwise you've left the asset out.`,
                    coach_r2_rd_economic:   `<strong>That's R&D treated as investment, with intent.</strong> Capitalising the durable portion on an economically justified life reveals reinvestment the income statement hid — and changes the economics you believe, which is the whole point of the adjustment.`,
                    coach_r2_rd_mechanical: `<strong>Why ten years?</strong> Capitalising all €71M over a textbook life is box-ticking. How much of that R&D built a durable asset versus maintained the platform? The adjustment only matters if it changes the reinvestment economics you actually believe.`,
                    coach_r2_rd_leave:      `<strong>Leaving R&D as expense penalises the company for investing.</strong> IFRS expensing depresses earnings and hides the asset the spend created. You don't have to capitalise mechanically — but you must reflect the intangible base it built.`,
                    coach_r2_double:        `<strong>You've counted the same cash flows twice.</strong> Valuing the customer base standalone AND applying a 22× revenue multiple that already prices in that retention inflates the value by hundreds of €millions. Intangibles live in cash flows — carry each through exactly one method.`,
                    coach_r2_neither:       `<strong>Dropping the intangible work overcorrects.</strong> Relying on the multiple alone imports the comparables' assumptions wholesale and abandons your own analysis of what makes Lumengrid special. The fix for double-counting is one route per intangible, not zero.`,
                    coach_r2_moat_high:     `<strong>That's an imagined moat.</strong> A double-digit durability premium says the growth barely fades for a decade — extraordinary, and exactly the kind of assumption a short-seller report would target. An intangible is only worth its cash-flow consequence; keep the premium defensible.`,
                    coach_r3_range:         `<strong>A range and an argument — good.</strong> Bear/base/bull with a named key-risk assumption is how you stay honestly “roughly right” when the stock trades. One number with no range gives you no way to be wrong gracefully.`,
                    coach_r3_point:         `<strong>One number, one method, no honest way to be roughly right.</strong> In six trading days the stock will trade somewhere. Without a bear and a bull case you can't tell the committee which assumption, if wrong, blows up your call.`,
                    coach_r3_anchor:        `<strong>You're back-solving to the bankers' range.</strong> Fitting your assumptions to land at €22–28 isn't valuation, it's reverse-engineering a conclusion — and it quietly surrenders the independence your clients pay for.`,
                    coach_r3_fade:          `<strong>Right key risk.</strong> Growth durability — the fade — is the biggest swing factor here and the assumption the moat is supposed to defend. Naming it, and stating your confidence in it, is the core of a defensible recommendation.`,
                    coach_r3_norisk:        `<strong>No base case is solid enough to stand alone.</strong> Declaring there's no key risk is how a single wrong assumption quietly destroys a valuation. Name the one that moves the answer most — almost certainly the growth fade.`,
                    coach_r3_extreme:       `<strong>Those assumptions are doing a lot of work.</strong> An aggressive WACC, terminal growth or margin can manufacture almost any value. Keep each within a defensible band, or the range becomes an argument you can't win in front of the committee.`,
                    coach_r4_retest:        `<strong>You let the new information move the model.</strong> A rival platform pressures the fade — your biggest driver — and an 8%-revenue customer reviewing its contract is real concentration risk. Re-testing and re-publishing is exactly right: assumptions are live, not frozen.`,
                    coach_r4_holdnews:      `<strong>You can't publish as if nothing changed.</strong> The competitor and the customer review both hit the growth-durability assumption that drove your number. Holding the same valuation tells the committee you weren't really watching the risk you named.`,
                    coach_r4_panic:         `<strong>Slashing to the DCF floor overreacts.</strong> One competitor launch and one contract review don't erase a 124%-retention moat. Reflect the news in the fade and the range — don't abandon the intangible thesis you built over three rounds.`,
                    coach_r4_independent:   `<strong>That's the only answer that protects the franchise.</strong> You published what the model supports and documented the independence. Under MiFID II — and for the institutional clients who actually pay you — credibility is the one asset you can't rebuild once it's spent.`,
                    coach_r4_soften:        `<strong>You've spent your credibility.</strong> Your model says hold and you've drifted to “buy” at €28 because a banker hinted. If the stock then trades at €19, the clients who pay for your research — and the regulator — will remember the conflict, not the relationship.`,
                    coach_r4_withdraw:      `<strong>Withdrawing dodges the question.</strong> Avoiding the conflict by dropping coverage protects you from the banker but abandons the clients who needed the call before the book closed. Independence means publishing the honest number, not disappearing.`,
                    coach_r4_holdcall:      `<strong>The rating follows the model.</strong> With your central estimate near the lower-mid of the range and fresh downside news, hold is the call you can defend — and you held it without flinching at the banking pressure. That's a recommendation the committee can stand behind.`,
                    coach_r4_buyhigh:       `<strong>Buy at the top doesn't match your own number.</strong> Your central estimate doesn't reach the high end of the range, yet you're rating buy. Either the model is wrong or the rating is — and a rating that ignores the model is the fastest way to lose credibility.`,
                    coach_r4_sellanchor:    `<strong>Sell contradicts your valuation.</strong> Your own estimate sits inside the range, so a sell call reads as the book-value scepticism resurfacing. Let the rating follow the number you actually believe.`,

