                    insight_strong:         `Street-leading call: you turned an off-balance-sheet, intangible-rich business into a defensible valuation — triangulated methods, a stress-tested range, a named key risk and an independent rating. You'll be judged “roughly right” even if the exact price differs.`,
                    insight_compromised:    `Rigorous but compromised: the analysis was sound, but you let the rating drift under pressure. The valuation may hold; the credibility behind it won't — and credibility is the asset the recommendation depends on.`,
                    insight_weak:           `The note isn't defensible: the valuation leaned too hard on a single lens, an under-stressed range, or book-value scepticism. When the stock trades, you'll have no honest way to have been roughly right.`,
                    insight_double:         `You double-counted intangible value — pricing the customer base or R&D both standalone and inside a multiple. That inflated the number by hundreds of €millions and is the first thing a sharp committee will unpick.`,
                    insight_point:          `Assumption robustness stayed low: a valuation is a range and an argument, not a point. Without a stress-tested bear/base/bull and a named key risk, one wrong assumption quietly destroys the call.`,
                    insight_lowcred:        `Credibility is damaged — most often by softening the rating under banking pressure or by a number you couldn't defend. It is the one asset an analyst owns, and the slowest to rebuild.`,
                    insight_inrange:        `Your central estimate landed inside the €1.4–1.9bn IPO range — and, done honestly rather than by back-solving, that means your intangible-aware case reconciled with where the market priced the deal.`,

                    hint_double:            `You're double-counting intangible value — carry the customer base and R&D through exactly one method, not both standalone and inside the multiple.`,
                    hint_cred:              `Credibility is dangerously low. Protect your independence and let the rating follow the model — it's the one asset you can't quickly rebuild.`,
                    hint_robust:            `Your valuation is too point-like. Build a bear/base/bull range and name the single assumption — almost certainly the growth fade — that carries the most risk.`,
                    hint_rigour:            `Valuation quality is weak — you may be anchoring on book value or one method. Frame the intangibles explicitly and triangulate DCF, multiples and a cross-check.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — rigorous, well-stressed and credible. Now keep the rating consistent with your central estimate and defend it without flinching.`,

